        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Atbfs2cjwM58gN2Ry6N3ag",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/backpressure.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Backpressure\\ndescription: A mechanism by which slow downstream consumers signal upstream producers to reduce emission rates, preventing unbounded buffer growth and memory exhaustion.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Backpressure\\n  - concept/Streaming-Systems\\n  - concept/Flow-Control\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single authoritative source describing established pattern; no contradictions.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nBackpressure in a streaming pipeline is the mechanism by which a slow downstream consumer signals an upstream producer to reduce its emission rate, rather than letting an unbounded buffer grow until the process runs out of memory.\\n\\n## Common Implementations\\n\\nSeveral established patterns implement backpressure:\\n\\n- **Bounded queues**: The producer writes to a queue that has a fixed capacity. Once the queue is full, the producer blocks until the consumer drains buffered items, creating a natural pause signal.\\n\\n- **Credit-based flow control**: The consumer explicitly grants the producer permission to send a specific number of items (N). The producer respects this credit limit and does not emit more until the consumer grants additional credits, allowing fine-grained control over emission rates.\\n\\n- **Reactive-pull models**: Instead of the producer pushing items to the consumer, the consumer requests items on its own schedule. The producer only emits in response to these requests, giving the consumer natural rate control.\\n\\n## Purpose\\n\\nBackpressure prevents memory exhaustion by ensuring that producers do not outpace consumers. Without it, buffers between pipeline stages can grow unbounded, consuming available memory until the system crashes or performs poorly.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: eval://catalog-description-specificity/sample-03\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
